messages:
jamie: heads-up: gitops started flipping our GPU capacity annotation for the model-serving chart during a canary in eu-west, autoscaler reacted and added nodes

maria: ouch. which annotation exactly and where is it defined?

jamie: `redwood.com/gpu-capacity: \"1\"` — it's emitted from charts/common/_helpers.tpl via our annotations helper

jamie: repro sequence: overlay sets `extraAnnotations`, helper does `merge` with `.Values.podAnnotations`, `toYaml` then output. sometimes `extraAnnotations` is `null` and the rendered output changes (quotes, ordering) so argo thinks there's a diff

ryan: is the value flipping or just rendering differences (ordering/quoting)? autoscaler shouldn't act on ordering alone

jamie: both. seen cases where unquoted 1 becomes quoted "1" and cases where annotations block flattens and we end up duplicating GPU tolerations in podSpec -> autoscaler interprets it as pod not tolerating GPU taints and scales GPUs down then back up

sasha: can you paste the helper?

jamie: simplified helper:
```
{{- define "common.annotations" -}}
{{- $a := merge .Values.podAnnotations .Values.extraAnnotations }}
{{- toYaml $a | nindent 8 }}
{{- end -}}
```
when `.Values.extraAnnotations` is `null` merge returns weird nil/empty behavior

maria: we've hit similar with terraform where gpu_count is sometimes int vs string in state, and helm's quoting makes diffs noisy. types are killing us

ryan: suggestion: coalesce both sides to dict and use a deterministic merge (we have `mergeOverwrite` or a `safeMerge` helper). also explicitly cast known keys (gpu-capacity) to string via `quote` to avoid int-string churn

jamie: proposed patch:
```
{{- $base := .Values.podAnnotations | default dict }}
{{- $extra := .Values.extraAnnotations | default dict }}
{{- $merged := mergeOverwrite $base $extra }}
{{- /* ensure gpu-capacity always string */ -}}
{{- if $merged.redwood_com_gpu_capacity }}
{{- $merged.redwood_com_gpu_capacity = (printf "%v" $merged.redwood_com_gpu_capacity) }}
{{- end }}
{{- toYaml $merged | nindent 8 }}
```
this avoids nil -> {} churn and stabilizes types/order

sasha: long term we should move the capacity signal to nodePool labels + nodeSelector/tolerations and let terraform own it. Helm should not be the source of truth for infra capacity metadata

maria: agreed, but immediate: add helm unit tests rendering with `extraAnnotations: null` and `extraAnnotations: {}` and assert no diff. also add a helm-lint rule that flags merges where types could change for known infra keys

ryan: opened WIP PR with helper change + tests: https://github.com/redwood-inference/charts/pull/482 — CI adding unit tests now

jamie: i'll run the gitops canary after CI passes and watch the autoscaler for 10m. if any unexpected scaling, i'll roll back the canary immediately

deploy-bot: Canary deployment started for chart model-serving v2.3.4-canary by @ryan :rocket:

sasha: also proposing a tf-plan gate in the gitops pipeline that checks node pool labels vs chart intent, prevents the helm->tf drift loop

maria: adding infra ticket and tagging infra-oncall for follow-up

jamie: thanks all — ping me if you see any weird scaling during the canary and i'll revert. :thumbsup:
